# Bridging Prediction and Decision

* Introduction

- The previous lesson catalogued four costs of ignoring causality, uncertainty,
  the business objective, and dynamics
- This lesson builds the causal and probabilistic tools that address the first
  two
- The business framing stays fixed
  - a prediction says what will happen
  - a decision says what to do
- This chapter goes underneath that framing to the machinery that makes causal
  claims precise enough to act on:
  - The ladder of causation, formalizing what _"what if"_ and _"why"_ mean
  - Causal graphs and identification, turning a causal question into a
    computable quantity
  - A probabilistic toolkit, turning a point estimate into a posterior a
    decision-maker can trust

* Prediction vs Decision, Formally
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:68 '* Prediction vs. Decision: Side-by-Side'

- Prediction and decision live in different mathematical settings; collapsing
  them is a common source of costly errors
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Aspect", "Prediction", "Decision"),
  rows: (
    ("Target", "E[Y | X]", "argmax_a E[U(Y) | do(a)]"),
    ("Data", "Observational only", "Observational + experimental"),
    ("Metric", "Accuracy, AUC, MSE", "Regret, expected utility"),
    ("Assumption", "i.i.d., stable world", "Models the intervention"),
    ("World", "No feedback", "Actions change the world"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
- @Key idea@: a decision target contains a $do(\cdot)$ that a predictive
  target never does; everything in this lesson exists to compute that
  $do(\cdot)$ from data

# The Ladder of Causation

## Three Rungs of Causal Reasoning

* The Ladder of Causation
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.2-Causal_AI_concepts.smd:12 '* The Ladder of Causation'
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:105 '- [P009] Pearl, "The Seven Pillars of Causal Reasoning with Reflections on Machine Learning", 2021'

- Pearl organizes causal reasoning into three rungs of increasing power
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Rung", "Symbol", "Activity", "Question"),
  rows: (
    ("1. Association", "Pr(Y | X)", "Seeing", "What is?"),
    ("2. Intervention", "Pr(Y | do(X), Z)", "Doing", "What if?"),
    ("3. Counterfactual", "Pr(Y_x | x', y')", "Imagining", "Why?"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
- @Key idea@: a model that only fits $\Pr(Y | X)$ lives on rung 1; no
  amount of rung-1 data answers a rung-2 or rung-3 question

* Rung 1: Association
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.2-Causal_AI_concepts.smd:33 '* Rung 1: Association'

- @Question@: how would seeing $X$ change your belief in $Y$?
- @Math@: $\Pr(Y | X)$
- Passive observation of whether $X$ and $Y$ relate; traditional predictive
  ML lives here entirely
- @Example@: what does a symptom tell you about a disease? what does a
  poll tell you about an election?
- @Limitations@: cannot distinguish a true cause from a confounder or from
  reverse causation

* Rung 2: Intervention
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.2-Causal_AI_concepts.smd:48 '* Rung 2: Intervention'

- @Question@: what happens to $Y$ if I do $X$?
- @Math@: $\Pr(Y | do(X), Z)$
- Setting $X$ by external action, not by observation, requires a causal
  model or a controlled experiment
- @Example@: if we cut price 10%, what happens to units sold? if we ban
  sodas, what happens to obesity?
- @Key idea@: $do(X)$ severs $X$ from its usual causes, so the answer can
  differ sharply from what observation suggests

* Rung 3: Counterfactuals
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.2-Causal_AI_concepts.smd:64 '* Rung 3: Counterfactuals'

- @Question@: was it $X$ that caused $Y$, for this specific unit?
- @Math@: $\Pr(Y_x | x', y')$, the outcome had $X$ been $x$, given we
  observed $x', y'$
- Imagining an alternative to what actually happened; the highest rung,
  requiring a full structural model
- @Example@: would this customer have churned had we offered a discount?
  why did the campaign fail?
- @Interpretation@: attribution, blame, and credit all live on rung 3

* Seeing vs Doing: A Worked Example
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.4-Causal_networks.smd:256 '* Tornado Warning: Level 2 (Intervention)'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=52%}
- @Example@: a tornado-warning chain
  - $T$: tornado forms; $W$: warning issued
  - $A, B$: radio and TV broadcast; $R$: residents warned
  - Channels broadcast only on official order, so $R = A \lor B$
- @Rung 1 (seeing)@: observe radio $A$ broadcast $\implies$ TV $B$ also
  broadcast
  - Correlated through the common cause $W$
- @Rung 2 (doing)@: force $do(A = 1)$ $\implies$ expect $B$ did _not_
  broadcast
  - Setting $A$ removes the edge $W \to A$; nothing links $A$ to $B$ anymore
- @Key idea@: seeing $A$ and doing $A$ give opposite conclusions about $B$
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph Tornado_Warning {
    splines=true; nodesep=0.5; ranksep=0.5;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    T [label="Tornado forms (T)", fillcolor="#FFB3BA"];
    W [label="Warning issued (W)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    A [label="Radio broadcasts (A)", fillcolor="#FFF4A3"];
    B [label="TV broadcasts (B)", fillcolor="#CFECCF"];
    R [label="Residents warned (R)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    { rank=min;  T; }
    { rank=same; W; }
    { rank=same; A; B; }
    { rank=max;  R; }

    T -> W;
    W -> A;
    W -> B;
    A -> R;
    B -> R;
}
```
::::
:::

# Causal Graphical Models

## Structure and Variable Types

* Structural Causal Models
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.4-Causal_networks.smd:366 '* Structural Causal Model'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: a _structural causal model_ (SCM) writes each variable as a
  function of its direct causes and an exogenous noise term
  $$X_i = f_i(Parents(X_i), \varepsilon_i)$$
- An SCM answers all three rungs from one object:
  - Observation: $\Pr(Y | X = x)$
  - Intervention: $\Pr(Y | do(X = x))$
  - Counterfactual: $\Pr(Y_{X=x} | X = x')$
- @Example@: a garden network where $C$ (cloudy) drives $S$ (sprinkler) and
  $R$ (rain), which both drive $W$ (wet grass)
  $$\Pr(C,R,S,W) = \Pr(W|R,S)\Pr(S|C)\Pr(R|C)\Pr(C)$$
- @Key idea@: the structural equations, not just the shape of the graph,
  are what let an SCM simulate an intervention
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph Sprinkler {
    rankdir=TB; nodesep=0.5; ranksep=0.5;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    C [label="Cloudy (C)", fillcolor="#b3cde3"];
    S [label="Sprinkler (S)", fillcolor="#ccebc5"];
    R [label="Rain (R)", fillcolor="#ccebc5"];
    W [label="Wet Grass (W)", fillcolor="#decbe4"];

    C -> S; C -> R; S -> W; R -> W;
}
```
::::
:::

* Confounders: The Fork Structure
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.6-Causal_inference_intro.smd:831 '* Example: Are Consultants Worth It?'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: in a fork $X \leftarrow Z \rightarrow Y$, $Z$ is a
  _confounder_, a common cause of $X$ and $Y$
  - $X$ and $Y$ are dependent even though neither causes the other
  - Conditioning on $Z$ blocks the spurious association
- @Example@: does hiring expensive consultants improve future profits?
  - Past profits drive both the decision to hire consultants and future
    profits
  - Comparing consultant-hiring firms to others directly conflates the
    confounder with the effect
- @Key idea@: without conditioning on $Z$, a predictive model bakes in the
  spurious association between $X$ and $Y$
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph Consultancy {
    rankdir=LR; nodesep=0.6; ranksep=0.6;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    P [label="Past Profits", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    Co [label="Hire Consultants", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    F [label="Future Profits", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    P -> Co; P -> F; Co -> F;
}
```
::::
:::

* Mediators: The Chain Structure
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.4-Causal_networks.smd:741 '* Mediator Variable'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: in a chain $X \rightarrow M \rightarrow Y$, $M$ is a
  _mediator_ that transmits the causal effect of $X$ on $Y$
  - Causation flows only in the direction of the arrows; association flows
    both ways
  - Conditioning on $M$ blocks the association: $(X \perp Y) | M$
- @Example@: a training program raises productivity through job
  satisfaction
  - _Indirect effect_: training $\to$ satisfaction $\to$ productivity
  - _Direct effect_: any remaining path from training to productivity not
    through satisfaction
- @Key idea@: unlike a confounder, a mediator should _not_ be conditioned
  on when the goal is the total effect of $X$ on $Y$
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph Mediator {
    rankdir=LR; nodesep=0.6; ranksep=0.6;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    X [label="Training Program (X)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    M [label="Job Satisfaction (M)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Y [label="Productivity (Y)", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];

    X -> M; M -> Y; X -> Y [style=dashed];
}
```
::::
:::

* Colliders Revisited: A Formal Treatment
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.4-Causal_networks.smd:1089 '* Collider'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.6-Causal_inference_intro.smd:973 '* D-separation'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: in $X \rightarrow M \leftarrow Y$, $M$ is a _collider_;
  $X$ and $Y$ start independent
  - Conditioning on $M$, or on any descendant of $M$, makes $X$ and $Y$
    dependent, a mechanism called _explaining away_
- @Example@: exercise and diet both affect body weight, which affects
  heart disease
  - Exercise and diet are independent among the general population
  - Among people at a _given_ body weight, less exercise implies a stricter
    diet, and vice versa
- @Definition@: a path is blocked given a conditioning set $Z$ if it
  contains a non-collider in $Z$, or a collider with no descendant in $Z$
  - Two variables are _d-separated_, and hence independent, if every path
    between them is blocked
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph Collider {
    rankdir=TB; nodesep=0.6; ranksep=0.6;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    E [label="Exercise (E)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    D [label="Diet (D)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    W [label="Body Weight (W)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    H [label="Heart Disease (H)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    { rank=same; E; D; }
    E -> W; D -> W; W -> H;
}
```
::::
:::

* Building a Causal DAG from Domain Knowledge
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.4-Causal_networks.smd:660 '* Building a Causal DAG'

- Nodes are like nouns (price, sales, revenue); edges are like verbs
  (causes, mediates)
- Every variable is both:
  - _Observed_ or _unobserved_, depending on whether it is measured in the
    data
  - _Endogenous_ or _exogenous_, depending on whether the model explains it
    or takes it as given
- @Example@: modeling income
  - Economic policy (exogenous, observed) and natural talent (exogenous,
    unobserved) both drive education and income
  - Education and income (endogenous, observed) are the targets of
    prediction and intervention
- @Key idea@: an unobserved confounder does not disappear because it is
  unmeasured; it still has to be represented in the graph, or the
  identification step in the next section will silently assume it away

# Identifying Causal Effects

## From Association to Intervention

* The Do-Operator: Simulating Intervention
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.5-Do_calculus.smd:79 '* The Do-Operator'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: $do(X = x)$ overrides $X$'s structural equation, replacing
  $X = f_X(Parents(X), \varepsilon_X)$ with the fixed value $x$
  - Deletes every incoming edge to $X$, producing a _mutilated_ graph
- Observing $X = x$ updates belief about $X$'s causes; $do(X = x)$ severs
  them, so it does not
  $$\Pr(Y | do(X=x)) \neq \Pr(Y | X=x)$$
- @Example@: $do(Sprinkler = true)$ removes the edge $Cloudy \to
  Sprinkler$
  - Only the descendants of $Sprinkler$ (here, $WetGrass$) are affected
  - $Cloudy$ and $Rain$ are untouched
- @Key idea@: the do-operator turns a graph into a simulator of
  interventions the business has not yet tried
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph DoOperator {
    rankdir=TB; nodesep=0.6; ranksep=0.6;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    C [label="Cloudy (C)", fillcolor="#b3cde3"];
    S [label="do(Sprinkler = true)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    R [label="Rain (R)", fillcolor="#ccebc5"];
    W [label="Wet Grass (W)", fillcolor="#decbe4"];

    C -> R; R -> W; S -> W;
}
```
::::
:::

* Randomized Controlled Trials: The Gold Standard
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.5-Do_calculus.smd:167 '* Randomized Controlled Trials'

- @Key idea@: randomizing $X$ is the physical way to perform $do(X)$
  - Random assignment severs every incoming edge into $X$, known and
    unknown alike
  - It turns the observational $\Pr(Y | X)$ into a valid estimate of
    $\Pr(Y | do(X))$
- @Example@: randomly assigning students to a tutoring program, instead of
  letting them opt in, removes the self-selection that would otherwise
  confound tutoring with prior motivation
- @Limitations@: a randomized controlled trial is not always available
  - May be unethical, e.g., assigning a harmful exposure
  - May be expensive, slow, or subject to non-compliance and attrition
- When a trial is not possible, the graph has to supply the identification a
  trial would have given for free

* The Back-Door Criterion
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.5-Do_calculus.smd:368 '* Backdoor Criteria'
// From: book_springer/related_books.md:4 '- [B002] Pearl et al., "The Book of Why", 2018'

- @Problem@: can $\Pr(Y | do(X))$ be computed from purely observational
  data, without ever randomizing $X$?
- @Definition@: a _backdoor path_ from $X$ to $Y$ is any path starting
  with an arrow into $X$
  - These paths carry non-causal association and have to be blocked
- @Definition@: a set $Z$ satisfies the _backdoor criterion_ for
  $(X, Y)$ if no variable in $Z$ is a descendant of $X$, and $Z$ blocks
  every backdoor path
- @Theorem@: if $Z$ satisfies the backdoor criterion, the adjustment
  formula holds
  $$\Pr(Y | do(X)) = \sum_z \Pr(Y | X, Z=z)\Pr(Z=z)$$
- @Key idea@: this formula turns an intervention, rung 2, into a
  computation over purely observational data, rung 1

* Back-Door Adjustment: A Worked Example
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.5-Do_calculus.smd:227 '* Backdoor Paths: Example'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Example@: estimating the causal effect of price on sales
  - Advertising spend is a confounder: it can raise the price a product can
    command _and_ raise sales directly
  - The backdoor path is $Price \leftarrow AdSpend \to Sales$
- Conditioning on advertising spend blocks that path
  $$\Pr(Sales | do(Price)) = \sum_a \Pr(Sales | Price, AdSpend=a)\Pr(AdSpend=a)$$
- @Common mistakes@: conditioning on a descendant of $X$, or on a
  mediator, biases the estimate instead of fixing it
  - The backdoor criterion says precisely which variables to condition on,
    not "condition on everything"
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph BackDoor {
    rankdir=TB; nodesep=0.6; ranksep=0.6;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    Ad [label="Ad Spend", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Pr [label="Price", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Sa [label="Sales", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    Ad -> Pr; Ad -> Sa; Pr -> Sa;
}
```
::::
:::

* When Back-Door Fails: The Front-Door Criterion
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.5-Do_calculus.smd:563 '* Front-Door Adjustment: Definition'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Problem@: sometimes no observable set blocks every backdoor path,
  because the confounder itself is unobserved
- @Definition@: the _frontdoor criterion_ identifies the effect through a
  mediator $M$ when
  1. All paths from $X$ to $Y$ go through $M$
  2. No unobserved confounder affects both $X$ and $M$
  3. $X$ blocks every backdoor path from $M$ to $Y$
- @Example@: does watching ads $(X)$ increase cereal purchases $(Y)$?
  - An unobserved factor, how much parents care about breakfast $(U)$,
    could drive both
  - Ads work through a mediator: they make kids nag $(M)$ for cereal
  - $U$ does not affect nagging, only ads do, so the frontdoor criterion
    holds
    $$\Pr(Y | do(X)) = \sum_m \Pr(M=m|X)\sum_{x'}\Pr(Y|M=m,X=x')\Pr(X=x')$$
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph FrontDoor {
    rankdir=LR; nodesep=0.6; ranksep=0.6;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    U [label="Care About Breakfast (U)", fillcolor="#ccebc5", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    X [label="Watch Ads (X)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    M [label="Kids Nag (M)", fillcolor="#decbe4"];
    Y [label="Buy Cereal (Y)", fillcolor="#fed9a6"];

    U -> X [style=dashed]; U -> Y [style=dashed]; X -> M; M -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

* Instrumental Variables: A Third Path
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.2-Causal_Inference_for_Time_Series.smd:198 '* Instrumental Variables (IV)'

- @Definition@: an _instrumental variable_ $Z$ affects the treatment $X$
  but has no path to the outcome $Y$ except through $X$
  - Provides exogenous variation in $X$ that stands in for a randomized
    trial
- @Example@: to estimate the effect of schooling on earnings, distance to
  the nearest college affects how much schooling someone gets, but has no
  direct effect on their earnings
- @Limitations@: a credible instrument is hard to find, and the estimate
  applies only to the units whose treatment actually shifts with the
  instrument
- @Key idea@: backdoor adjustment, frontdoor adjustment, and
  instrumental variables are three different ways of turning the same
  problem, identifying $\Pr(Y | do(X))$, into a computation over
  observational data

# Potential Outcomes and Treatment Effects

## Measuring What Cannot Be Observed

* Potential Outcomes: Rubin's Framework
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.6-Causal_inference_intro.smd:283 '* Potential Outcomes'
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:4 '- [P063] Rubin, "Estimating causal effects of treatments in randomized and nonrandomized studies", 1974'

- @Definition@: unit $i$ has two _potential outcomes_ under a binary
  treatment: $Y_{1i}$ if treated, $Y_{0i}$ if not
  - Pearl's notation: $Y_i | do(T_i = t)$; Rubin's notation: $Y_{t,i}$
  - Exactly one is observed, or "factual"; the other is "counterfactual"
    $$Y_i = T_i \cdot Y_{1i} + (1 - T_i) \cdot Y_{0i}$$
- @Fact@: the _fundamental problem of causal inference_ is that $Y_{1i}$
  and $Y_{0i}$ can never both be observed for the same unit
- @Example@: a business either cuts prices or does not in a given week;
  the amount it would have sold under the other choice is never recorded
- @Key idea@: causal inference estimates population quantities like
  $\EE[Y_1 - Y_0]$ without ever observing an individual $Y_1 - Y_0$

* The Bias Equation
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.6-Causal_inference_intro.smd:391 '* The Confounding Bias Equation'

- @Problem@: the quantity we can measure, $\EE[Y|T=1] - \EE[Y|T=0]$, is
  not automatically the causal effect $\EE[Y_1 - Y_0]$
- @Fact@: the two decompose as
  $$
  \underbrace{\EE[Y|T=1]-\EE[Y|T=0]}_{\text{measured}}
  = \underbrace{\EE[Y_1-Y_0|T=1]}_{\text{ATT}}
  + \underbrace{\EE[Y_0|T=1]-\EE[Y_0|T=0]}_{\text{bias}}
  $$
- @Interpretation@: association equals causation only when the bias term
  vanishes, i.e., when treated and untreated units are _exchangeable_ but
  for the treatment
- @Example@: businesses that cut prices likely differ from those that do
  not, in size, location, or timing, before any price cut happens; the bias
  term captures exactly that pre-existing difference
- @Key idea@: randomization is the one intervention guaranteed to zero out
  the bias term, by construction

* Average and Conditional Treatment Effects
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.6-Causal_inference_intro.smd:326 '* Average Treatment Effect'

- @Definition@: the _average treatment effect_ is the population-level
  impact of treatment
  $$ATE \defeq \EE[Y_1 - Y_0] = \EE[Y|do(T=1)] - \EE[Y|do(T=0)]$$
- @Definition@: the _average treatment effect on the treated_ restricts to
  units that actually received treatment
  $$ATT \defeq \EE[(Y_1-Y_0)|T=1]$$
- @Definition@: the _conditional average treatment effect_ restricts to a
  subgroup defined by covariates $X=x$
  $$CATE \defeq \EE[(Y_1-Y_0)|X=x]$$
- @Example@: the average treatment effect of a price cut answers _"should
  we roll this out to everyone?"_; the conditional average treatment effect
  answers _"which customers should get the discount?"_
- @Key idea@: CATE turns a single population-wide answer into a
  personalized policy

* Assumptions Are Not Free
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.6-Causal_inference_intro.smd:581 '* Independence Property (Aka Unconfoundedness)'
// From: book_springer/related_books.md:15 '- [B050] Hernán & Robins, "Causal Inference: What If", 2020'

- Every identification result in this lesson, backdoor, frontdoor, or
  randomization, rests on assumptions that do not verify themselves from
  data
- @Positivity@: every covariate group must have a positive chance of
  receiving each treatment, $0 < \Pr(T | X) < 1$
  - Without it, some units have no comparable counterfactual in the data at
    all
- @Ignorability@: given the adjustment set, potential outcomes are
  independent of treatment, $(Y_0, Y_1) \perp T | X$
  - Fails silently when a relevant confounder is missing from $X$
- @Key idea@: a causal estimate is only as credible as the graph and the
  assumptions behind it; correct arithmetic on a wrong graph still produces
  a wrong answer

# Toward Probabilistic Decision-Making

## Why Point Estimates Are Not Enough

* Posteriors, Not Point Estimates
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson07.2-Posterior_Based_Decisions.smd:12 '* Posterior-Based Decisions'

- Point estimates hide how much a business could be wrong, and conflate
  irreducible randomness with what more data could fix
- @Definition@: a _Bayesian_ treatment replaces a point estimate
  $\hat\theta$ with a posterior distribution
  $$\Pr(\theta | \mathcal{D}) \propto \Pr(\mathcal{D} | \theta) \Pr(\theta)$$
- The posterior's spread _is_ the epistemic uncertainty; a separate noise
  term in the structural equation captures the aleatoric part
- @Key idea@: a causal effect estimated from a backdoor or frontdoor
  adjustment is not one number, it is a posterior over that number, and the
  width of that posterior is itself decision-relevant

* Variational Inference: Scaling Bayesian Updating
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.2-Probabilistic_deep_learning.smd:1899 '* Variational Inference (VI)'

- @Problem@: an exact posterior $\Pr(\theta | \mathcal{D})$ is rarely
  available in closed form once the model has any real complexity
- @Definition@: _variational inference_ replaces exact inference with
  optimization
  - Pick a tractable family of distributions $q_\phi(\theta)$
  - Fit $\phi$ to minimize the distance to the true posterior, by
    maximizing the evidence lower bound (ELBO)
- @Key idea@: variational inference turns "compute the posterior" into
  "solve an optimization problem," which scales to the large models this
  book eventually needs
- @Remark@: amortized inference and gradient estimation for variational
  inference are developed in full in Part II

* Normalizing Flows: Flexible Posteriors
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.2-Probabilistic_deep_learning.smd:1754 '* Flow-Based Generative Models (Normalizing Flows)'

- @Problem@: a variational family that is too simple, e.g., a single
  Gaussian, can badly underfit a posterior with skew or multiple modes
- @Definition@: a _normalizing flow_ builds a flexible distribution by
  chaining invertible transformations of a simple base distribution
  - Exact density evaluation and exact sampling, unlike many other flexible
    generative models
- @Key idea@: normalizing flows let the shape of the posterior match the
  shape of the actual uncertainty, instead of forcing it into a fixed
  parametric form
- @Remark@: normalizing flows are developed in full in Part II, alongside
  variational inference

* Calibration and Conformal Prediction
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.2-Probabilistic_deep_learning.smd:1862 '* Calibration of Neural Networks'
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:49 '- [P069] Lakshminarayanan et al., "Simple and Scalable Predictive Uncertainty Estimation using Deep Ensembles", 2017'

- @Definition@: a model is _calibrated_ if its stated confidence matches
  its actual accuracy
  - A poorly calibrated posterior is worse than an honest point estimate: it
    looks trustworthy while being wrong
- @Definition@: _conformal prediction_ wraps any model with a
  model-agnostic procedure that produces prediction sets with a guaranteed
  coverage rate
  - E.g., a 90% conformal interval contains the true outcome at least 90% of
    the time, under minimal assumptions
- @Key idea@: calibration and conformal prediction let a decision-maker
  trust the width of an interval, not just its center

* Decision Readiness Under Uncertainty
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.6-Causal_inference_intro.smd:581 '* Independence Property (Aka Unconfoundedness)'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:1076 '* Aleatoric vs Epistemic Uncertainty'

- @Problem@: not every causal claim is safe to act on, even with a
  posterior attached
- A claim is _decision-ready_ when three conditions hold together:
  - The identification assumptions, positivity and ignorability, are
    plausible for the population being acted on
  - The posterior's epistemic width is narrow enough that the recommended
    action is stable across its range
  - The aleatoric floor, the part no amount of data removes, is small
    relative to the stakes of the decision
- @Key idea@: a wide posterior is not a reason to ignore uncertainty, it
  is the signal to gather more data before acting, rather than after

# Roadmap

* Causal Models as the Foundation for Decisions
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:199 '* Causal Models for Decisions'

- A causal model built with the tools in this lesson supplies exactly what a
  decision needs and a predictive model cannot:
  - The interventional distribution $\Pr(Y | do(a))$, from identification
  - A posterior over that distribution, from the probabilistic toolkit
- @Example@: a marketing team can observe that people who open a
  promotional email buy more
  - Only a causal model, identified through the right adjustment set, says
    whether _sending_ the email causes the purchase
- @Key idea@: for decisions, the interventional layer is not optional
  detail, it is the layer prediction alone cannot supply

* From Identification to Decisions

- The pipeline built in this lesson feeds directly into the next step of the
  book
  $$
  data \to \text{causal DAG} \to \text{identification}
       \to \Pr(Y|do(a)) \to \text{posterior}
       \to \argmax_a \EE[U(Y)|do(a)]
  $$
- Everything before the last arrow is this lesson; the last arrow, turning
  an identified, uncertain effect into a choice of action, is decision
  theory
- @Key idea@: identification answers _"can we know the effect?"_; decision
  theory, next, answers _"given what we know, what should we do?"_

* Roadmap: What Comes Next

- This lesson turned the ladder of causation into computable identification
  results, and turned point estimates into posteriors
- The next lesson closes Part I by turning those interventional, uncertain
  effects into decisions:
  - Utility functions and expected utility under $do(a)$
  - Cost asymmetry and multi-objective trade-offs
  - Dynamics: what happens when the world responds to the decision itself
- @Key idea@: identification and uncertainty quantification are necessary
  for a good decision, but not sufficient; the business objective still has
  to enter the picture
